THE PER-CALL ENV-VAR QUESTION IS A MEASURED NULL — `getenv` fires **29 times in
a whole photo_cid 512x512 p6 encode**, 30 on the screenshot and 32 at preset 2,
against 133,020 `malloc`s and 110,747 `calloc`s in the same encode. The two
uncached `std::env::var("SVTAV1_SC_TOOLS")` reads the brief named are not on the
per-superblock path: they sit in `encode_tile_rows`, which runs **once per
tile**. Nothing was changed for them. What IS a per-call cost the C encoder does
not pay is the ALLOCATOR: the port made **244,967 heap allocations per encode
against C's 1,228 (199x)**, and the whole `malloc`/`calloc`/`free`/`realloc`
family cost **82.25 M inclusive Ir = 5.44 % of the frame against C's 0.14 %
(85x)**. That is also the answer to the LAYOUT question — the port's hot
structures are not materially bigger than C's field-for-field (`BlkStruct` 400 B
vs `BlockDecision` 288 B; `MbModeInfo` 60 B vs `MvpMiEntry` 20 B; the port is
SMALLER on both), they differ in OWNERSHIP: C's `ModeDecisionCandidate` is 168
bytes of POD whose pixel and coefficient storage is a pool allocated once per
encoder handle (`md_process.c:585-601`), while the port's `leaf_funnel::Cand` is
528 bytes of which **360 are sixteen `Vec`/`Option<Vec>` headers**, each one its
own `malloc`/`free` per candidate per block.

Four sites were fixed. Together they remove **86,554 of the 244,967 blocks
(-35.3 %) and 42.2 MB of the 106.2 MB (-39.8 %)**, for **-3.162 % Ir at
photo_cid p6, -4.838 % on the screenshot and -0.823 % at p2**, and a wall clock
of **1.018x-1.029x on four independent cells, every one of them with a span
disjoint from its OWN same-binary control**. Peak heap is a NULL
(6,223,973 -> 6,230,148 B, +0.099 %). Every cell byte-identical on both ISAs.

**The single biggest one was not in any previous ranking and it is worth stating
on its own: a per-superblock range-coder output buffer was sized to the WHOLE
FRAME.** `pipeline.rs:13504` built the CDF-chain simulation writer with
`AomWriter::new(w * h * 2 + 256)` — 524,544 zeroed bytes, 64 times per
512x512 frame, **33,570,816 bytes of which DHAT measures 63,233 EVER WRITTEN and
2,425 EVER READ** (0.19 % and 0.007 % utilisation). It was 31.79 M Ir, 2.10 % of
the p6 frame, spent zeroing memory nothing reads. It is now sized to a
superblock.

date:   2026-09-05
hosts:  MEASUREMENT AND WALL CLOCK on `r7900x` (x86_64-linux, AMD Ryzen 9
        7900X, 24 cores). A SIBLING LANE WAS LIVE THROUGHOUT (`zenav1-svt--v4x1`
        running its own gates and a callgrind): two of twenty-four cores busy,
        load average 2.50 at the A/B launch, 1.01 at the callgrind launch.
        Callgrind and DHAT are deterministic and contention-immune by
        construction; the wall-clock A/B is protected instead by running its OWN
        same-binary control in the SAME session — see AB below.
        BYTE GATES on both: `r7900x` and the aarch64 Mac (M-series, macOS 25.5).
        The aarch64 box also had a sibling lane live (`zenav1-svt--mt1`), which
        is why no wall clock was taken there.
tools:  valgrind-3.26.0 --tool=callgrind (Ir, `--cache-sim=no --branch-sim=no`)
        and --tool=dhat (blocks / bytes / bytes-read / bytes-written per
        allocation site, `--num-callers=8`), callgrind_annotate,
        tools/perf_profile/{callcount.py,tree_callers.py}, tools/perf_ab.sh,
        gcc 15.2.0 for the C `sizeof` probe, rustc 1.98.1.
base:   `main@origin` 7ec5b5572. Candidate = that plus the four changes below.
        Both built `env -u RUSTFLAGS` (no `target-cpu=native`), `-j 4`,
        `nice -n 19`.
C:      reference/svt-av1 @ 3115c0c1 — the PREBUILT oracle at
        `rust/tools/perf_c_encode/perf_c_encode` (2026-09-03). NOT rebuilt.
        The `sizeof` probe compiles only the HEADERS
        (`-Wl,--unresolved-symbols=ignore-all`); it links nothing of the oracle
        and does not touch the build.
cells:  photo_cid 512x512 p2 and p6, screen_terminal 512x512 p6, qp 40, still,
        from `r7900x:~/tmp/rc1/yuv` — the same .yuv files the realimg and
        stall-attribution records used.
data:   benchmarks/percall_layout_2026-09-05.tsv (PERCALL / ENVSITE / STRUCTS /
        ALLOCTOT / SITES / IR / AB / MEM).

=================== BYTE-IDENTITY ===================

Every measured cell was `cmp`'d, and the C oracle's own .obu was `cmp`'d against
the port's on the two p6 cells:
  * photo_cid p6      45,688 B — port base == port candidate == C
  * screen_terminal p6 10,090 B — port base == port candidate == C
  * photo_cid p2      46,462 B — port base == port candidate
Those are the same byte counts the realimg and stall-attribution records
recorded at those cells.

aarch64 (Mac) and x86_64 (r7900x) gate results are in the GATES section below.

=================== 1. QUESTION A — PER-CALL COSTS, WITH CALL COUNTS ===========

Method: callgrind over ONE whole encode (warmup 0), `callcount.py --demangle`
for exact per-function call counts. The whole shipped surface
(`crates/{svtav1-encoder,svtav1-dsp,svtav1-types,svtav1-target}/src`) was swept
statically first; every candidate class was then priced by call count.

  class                              photo p6   screen p6   photo p2   verdict
  getenv (ALL sites)                       29          30         32   NULL
  malloc                              133,020     134,004  3,622,713   FIXED (part)
  calloc                              110,747     127,195  3,757,312   FIXED (part)
  free                                243,766     261,198  7,380,024   FIXED (part)
  realloc                               1,202       4,977     46,678   not addressed
  core::fmt::write                          3         n/m        n/m   NULL
  Mutex / RwLock                            0           0          0   NULL — none exist
  HashMap / BTreeMap                        0           0          0   NULL — none exist
  Instant::now / clock syscall              0           0          0   NULL
  file I/O inside the encode                0           0          0   NULL

**getenv is 29 calls, not 29 per block.** A 512x512 frame has 64 superblocks and
thousands of blocks; 29 < 64 already rules out a per-superblock read, and the
number barely moves between preset 2 (32) and preset 6 (29) — which it would
have to if it were keyed on MD work. **The brief's premise that the two
`SVTAV1_SC_TOOLS` reads "sit in the per-superblock funnel-config path" is
wrong**: their enclosing function is `encode_tile_rows` (the brief's line numbers
11213/11224 are also stale — they are 11599/11610 at this commit), which runs
once per tile, so those two reads fire ONCE EACH on a single-tile frame. No
change was made, `dbgenv.rs` was not extended, and the `SVTAV1_SC_TOOLS` tool
still works exactly as before because nothing about it was touched.

Two further corrections to the brief's candidate list, both from reading the
source rather than the grep line:
  * `leaf_funnel/predict.rs:251` is **already cached** — the `std::env::var`
    is the body of a `OnceLock::get_or_init` closure (`leaf_funnel::predict::dbg_xy`), the
    same shape `dbgenv.rs`'s `value_vars!` uses. So is
    `depth_refine.rs:1357` (`SVTAV1_DBG_MI`).
  * `pipeline.rs:8952` (`SVTAV1_INTERDBG`) IS per-block and IS uncached — but it
    is on the INTER arm, which a still/key frame never enters, so it took **zero
    calls in every cell measured**. It is the one env read that would be worth
    caching the day inter frames ship; it is left alone because caching it now
    would be an unmeasured change.

`Mutex`, `RwLock`, `HashMap`, `BTreeMap` and `Instant::now` are ABSENT from the
shipped encoder and DSP crates — that is a static fact from the sweep, not an
inference from the profile. The only atomics on a per-call path are
`leaf_funnel/coeff_rate.rs:40` (behind a `OnceLock` early-return) and
`pipeline.rs:11956` (`hbd_used.store`, bd10 only). `format!`/`to_string` appear
22+6 times in the encoder and 2+2 in the DSP, all on error/diagnostic paths, and
the profile agrees: three `core::fmt::write` calls per encode.

**So the whole of question A reduces to one class — the allocator — and the rest
is reported as a null.**

=================== 2. QUESTION B — LAYOUT, PORT vs C =========================

`sizeof` is IDENTICAL on x86_64-linux (gcc 15.2.0) and aarch64-macOS for every C
type below, so no ISA qualifier is needed.

  role                       C type                        C     port type                   port   port/C
  per-candidate MD state     ModeDecisionCandidate       168     leaf_funnel::types::Cand      528    3.14
  candidate storage          ModeDecisionCandidateBuffer 208     (folded into Cand)              -       -
  per-block committed state  BlkStruct                   400     partition::BlockDecision      288    0.72
  MD context                 ModeDecisionContext      16,368     rate_tables::MdRates       17,984    1.10
  neighbour / xd view        MacroBlockD                 184     partition::NeighborEdges      280    1.52
  mode-info cell             BlockModeInfo                36     intrabc_mvp::MvpMiEntry        20    0.56
  mode-info cell (full)      MbModeInfo                   60     intrabc_mvp::MvpMiEntry        20    0.33
  MV stack entry             CandidateMv                  12     (not materialised)              -       -

**The port is not systematically fatter.** It is SMALLER on the two structures
that are stored per 4x4 cell across a whole frame (`MvpMiEntry` 20 B against
`BlockModeInfo` 36 / `MbModeInfo` 60) and smaller on the per-block committed
state (288 vs 400). Its MD context is within 10 % of C's. **A "port struct
materially larger than C's costs more cache lines per block" is therefore NOT
what is happening**, which is consistent with the stall record's finding that the
port's L1D miss rate is HALF C's.

The one big ratio is `Cand` at 3.14x, and its cause is not padding or field
order — it is OWNERSHIP:

  * **16 of `Cand`'s fields are `Vec` or `Option`-of-`Vec`** (`pred`, `pred10`,
    `txb_q`, `txb_eob`, `txb_cul`, `txb_type`, `y_recon`, `y_recon10`,
    `u_recon10`, `v_recon10`, `y_recon_d0`, `u_q`, `v_q`, `u_recon`, `v_recon`,
    `palette`). Fifteen bare `Vec`s at 24 B each are **360 of the 528 bytes**
    (68 %). C's `ModeDecisionCandidate` holds none: its pixels and coefficients
    live in `ModeDecisionCandidateBuffer`'s POINTERS into a pool that
    `md_process.c:585-601` allocates ONCE per encoder handle and reuses for
    every block of every frame.
  * **`txb_q: Vec<Vec<i32>>` is the one genuine extra INDIRECTION LEVEL.** C
    indexes one flat `quant_coeff` buffer by a txb offset; the port does an outer
    load then an inner load, and allocates 1 + N blocks. DHAT counts 8,705
    `Vec<Vec<i32>>` drops per p6 encode.
  * `inter: Option<Box<InterCand>>` is 8 B in `Cand` and one more dependent load
    when taken — but `InterCand` is only 80 B and the box is `None` on every
    still frame, so this is not a still-path cost.

The measured consequence, and it is the whole of the layout answer:

  metric (one photo_cid 512x512 p6 encode)   port base    port cand         C
  heap blocks                                  244,967      158,413     1,228
  heap bytes                               106,170,317   63,966,573  24,112,240
  peak live bytes (t-gmax)                   6,223,973    6,230,148  19,243,442
  malloc+calloc+free+realloc inclusive Ir   82,254,098          n/m     966,326
    .. as share of the frame                    5.44 %          n/m      0.14 %

**199x C's allocation COUNT while using 0.32x C's peak live BYTES.** The port is
not using more memory; it is churning the allocator to hold less of it. Peak
live blocks tell the same story from the other side: 6,938 against C's 1,203.

And 199x understates it, because **every one of C's 1,228 allocations is a
`_ctor`** — DHAT over the same cell attributes them to
`picture_control_set_ctor` (172 blocks / 1.45 MB),
`svt_muxing_queue_ctor` (160), `svt_system_resource_ctor` (149),
`svt_aom_neighbor_array_unit_ctor` (116), `svt_av1_enc_init` (73),
`svt_aom_picture_parent_control_set_creator` (72),
`svt_aom_mode_decision_context_ctor` (53 / 1.19 MB),
`svt_picture_buffer_desc_ctor` (25 / 3.90 MB) and the rest of the same shape.
**C allocates nothing inside the encode loop.** The port's 244,967 are all
inside the frame. That is the pool (`md_process.c:585-601`) doing exactly what
its comment says, measured from the other end.

AoS-vs-SoA: no site was found where the port uses a struct-of-fields against C's
parallel arrays or the reverse on anything walked per block. The mode-info grid
is AoS on both sides (`MvpMiEntry` against `BlockModeInfo`), the candidate arrays
are AoS on both, and the coefficient planes are flat on both. **That axis is
reported as a null.**

`Option` discriminants: the stall record's `checked_sub` line (2.68 % of the p6
frame, 20.16 M conditional branches on `core/src/num/uint_macros.rs:1052`) is
NOT reducible to nine source sites — `crates/svtav1-encoder/src` contains 9
`checked_sub` and **69 `saturating_sub`**, plus 3 more `saturating_sub` in the
DSP, and both compile through the same `core` line. They cannot be separated by
source line (callgrind attributes the inlined `core` code to the `core` source
line, which is exactly why the stall record called that number a LOCALISATION
and not an overhead). **The census is reported; no `Option`-to-sentinel change
was attempted, because none of them could be priced individually.**

=================== 3. WHAT WAS FIXED, AND WHY EACH IS BYTE-INERT =============

All four are read-side or storage-shape changes. None touches arithmetic, a
coding decision, or a written byte.

1. **`pipeline.rs:13504` — the per-SB simulation writer is sized to a
   superblock, not the frame.** `AomWriter::new(w * h * 2 + 256)` ->
   `AomWriter::new(sb_size * sb_size * 2 + 256)`.
   Evidence it is inert: `OdEcEnc::new`'s own doc says "growth happens on demand,
   so any capacity — including 0 — is valid", and the buffer past `offs` is
   scratch that is never READ before it is written — `normalize` re-establishes
   `len >= offs + 8` with `resize(2 * len + 8, 0)` and then OVERWRITES eight
   bytes with `copy_from_slice`; `done` resizes to `offs + b` and ASSIGNS each
   byte; `propagate_carry_bwd` only touches indices strictly below `offs`.
   Measured waste removed: 33,570,816 B/frame allocated-and-zeroed of which
   63,233 B written and 2,425 B read.
   **AND THE SIZE DEPENDENCE IS QUADRATIC, WHICH THE 512x512 CELL HIDES:** the
   old capacity was `w * h * 2 + 256` per SB and the SB COUNT is also
   `w * h / sb_size^2`, so the per-frame zero-fill went as `w^2 * h^2`. At
   512x512 that is 33.5 MB; at 2048x2048 preset 6 it is
   1024 SBs x 8,388,864 B = **8.59 GB of `calloc` zero-fill per frame.** The new
   capacity is SB-sized, so the per-frame total is now linear in pixels
   (1024 x 8,448 B = 8.6 MB at 2048). Measured at 2048: see the BIG rows in the
   TSV.

2. **`leaf_funnel/predict.rs` — `hadamard_satd` / `hadamard_satd_hbd` take their
   residual and coefficient tiles from a thread-local `HadamardScratch`.**
   Evidence it is inert: the two `vec![0; tx*tx]` already sat OUTSIDE the tile
   loops and were fully overwritten by `residual_i16` / `aom_hadamard_*` on every
   tile, so tiles 2..N already ran against stale contents. Reusing them across
   CALLS is the same contract one level up. `try_borrow_mut` + closure hand-back
   is `tx_pipeline::with_txt_out`'s existing shape, so a re-entrant caller gets
   fresh buffers rather than a panic.
   Measured alone: -5,534,170 Ir at photo_cid p6 (-0.366 %), byte-identical.

3. **`partition.rs` — `extract_neighbors_tiled` returns a stack `NeighborEdges`
   instead of two `Vec<u8>`.** 44,502 blocks per p6 encode for 435,872 bytes —
   an average payload of **9.8 bytes per malloc/free pair**, the purest per-call
   cost in the profile. The struct carries `[u8; 128]` twice; the bound is AV1's
   `MAX_SB_SIZE` (128) and is stated by an `assert!` rather than a clamp,
   because a clamp would hand the predictor fewer reference samples than it
   indexes — the silent wrong-pixel failure the conformance mandate forbids.

4. **`leaf_funnel/overlay.rs` — `predict_unit_overlay` builds its canvas edges,
   above/left and `above_c` on the stack.** 30,285 blocks for 879,601 bytes (a
   29-byte average). The canvas' INTERIOR was never written and never read —
   only its top row and left column — so it is now two `[u8; 65]` arrays instead
   of a `(txw+1) * (txh+1)` heap allocation. Bounded by `TX_64X64`, again by
   `assert!`.

Not done, and named with their measured size so the next chunk can start from a
number rather than a guess: `mds3::eval_candidate` (40,223 blocks / 4.17 MB),
`tx_pipeline::tx_unit_inner` (26,580 / 11.38 MB — the residue is the OWNED
`TxUnitOut` `Vec`s, not the already-scratched interior), `txt_search::{closure#0}`
(22,617 / 4.57 MB, mostly `slice::to_vec`), `pd0::tx_quant_core` (16,320 /
11.01 MB, three owned `Vec`s returned per call from ONE caller), and
`inject_candidates` / `encode_block_syntax` / `evaluate_leaf` (9,416 / 9,416 /
8,239). Every one is the same shape: an owned-`Vec` RETURN where C writes into a
pool buffer. `pd0::quantize_b`'s outputs (`pd0.rs:726-727`) are explicitly NOT
in that list as a ZERO-FILL removal — DHAT shows only 1,177,220 of their
3,407,872 bytes written, so their zero-fill IS read; a scratch there must still
`fill(0)` and would save only the malloc/free, not the memset.

THE NEXT CHUNK, SIZED: **a `Pd0Scratch` thread-local, shaped exactly like
`tx_pipeline::TxScratch`.** The PD0 lvl-1 path allocates five buffers per call
from ONE caller chain (`lvl1_block_cost_rect` -> `lvl1_cost_from_pred` ->
`tx_quant_core` -> `quantize_b`), 5,440 calls per photo_cid p6 encode:

  pd0.rs:2294  pred      5,440 blocks   1,048,576 B   read = written (full)
  pd0.rs:2358  residual  5,440          4,194,304 B   read = written (full)
  pd0.rs:965   coeffs    5,440          4,194,304 B   4,448,256 written
  pd0.rs:726   qcoeff    5,440          3,407,872 B   1,177,220 written
  pd0.rs:727   dqcoeff   5,440          3,407,872 B   1,177,220 written
                        ------          -----------
                        27,200          16,252,928 B

`pred` and `residual` are FULLY overwritten before they are read (by
`inter_pred_into`/`predict_dc` and `residual_i32` respectively), so their
zero-fill is dead the same way `hadamard_satd`'s was. `tx_quant_core` returns
`(u16, u64, Vec<i32>, usize, Vec<i32>)` and both `Vec`s are consumed inside
`lvl1_cost_from_pred`, so an out-param signature is a local change — no
consumer outside pd0.rs sees it. That is 27,200 of the remaining 158,413 blocks
(17.2 %) behind one scratch struct and one signature change.

A REFINEMENT LEFT UNMEASURED, named so it is not mistaken for an oversight:
`NeighborEdges` is 280 bytes returned BY VALUE, of which only `width` +
`height` are meaningful (typically 8-16 on this content, per the 9.8-byte
average payload). LLVM returns it through an `sret` pointer, so the caller pays
the two `[u8; 128]` arrays' zero-init on every call. The net is already measured
as a WIN, so nothing further is claimed — but a caller-owned
`&mut NeighborEdges` reused across a block's TX units would remove even that,
and it is a smaller change than the pd0 scratch above.

=================== 4. THE NUMBERS ============================================

**Instructions** (callgrind, whole process, one encode, every cell
byte-identical; C's own .obu `cmp`'d against the port's on the two p6 cells):

  cell                       base Ir          cand Ir      delta   port/C base -> cand
  photo_cid 512x512 p6   1,511,752,954    1,463,950,401   -3.162 %   2.1425 -> 2.0748
  screen_terminal p6       893,970,089      850,717,449   -4.838 %   2.5155 -> 2.3939
  photo_cid 512x512 p2  45,564,135,018   45,189,114,595   -0.823 %   n/m

p2 gets only the three stack/scratch changes: the per-SB CDF-chain simulation
writer is an M4..M6 path (`update_cdf_level` 2), so preset 2 never builds it.

The `hadamard_satd` change was also measured ALONE before the others were added:
1,511,752,954 -> 1,506,218,784, **-5,534,170 Ir (-0.366 %)**, byte-identical.

**Allocations** (DHAT, photo_cid 512x512 p6, one encode):

  metric                          base          cand      delta        C
  heap blocks                  244,967       158,413    -35.33 %    1,228
  heap bytes               106,170,317    63,966,573    -39.75 %  24,112,240
  peak live bytes            6,223,973     6,230,148    +0.099 %  19,243,442
  peak live blocks               6,938         6,941    +0.043 %      1,203

**Wall clock** (`tools/perf_ab.sh`, 21 interleaved paired rounds, every row
ident=Y, each A/B with its OWN same-binary control in the same session):

  round  cell                 preset   A/B      control   corrected  speedup  disjoint
    1    photo_cid 512x512      2    0.9777     1.0024      0.9753    1.025    yes
    1    photo_cid 512x512      6    0.9843     1.0032      0.9811    1.019    yes
    1    screen_terminal        2    0.9843     1.0060      0.9784    1.022    yes
    1    screen_terminal        6    0.9730     0.9932      0.9797    1.021    yes
    2    photo_cid 512x512      2    0.9776     1.0023      0.9753    1.025    yes
    2    photo_cid 512x512      6    0.9827     1.0005      0.9822    1.018    yes
    2    screen_terminal        2    0.9830     1.0058      0.9773    1.023    yes
    2    screen_terminal        6    0.9762     1.0042      0.9721    1.029    yes

Round 1 is the three stack/scratch changes; round 2 adds the range-coder resize.
The p25/p75 spans are in the TSV; every A/B span is disjoint from its control's.

**A RESULT THAT LOOKS WRONG AND IS NOT, STATED BECAUSE IT IS THE INTERESTING
ONE: adding the range-coder resize TRIPLED the Ir saving at photo_cid p6
(-1.070 % -> -3.162 %) and did NOT move the wall clock.** Round 1 -> round 2,
per cell: photo p2 1.025 -> 1.025, photo p6 1.019 -> 1.018, screen p2
1.022 -> 1.023, screen p6 1.021 -> 1.029. Three cells are flat; the fourth
moves by 0.8 pp, which is inside round 1's own span there (its A/B read
0.9588-0.9874 against a 0.9850-1.0082 control — the widest of the eight rows).
**No cell shows the resize as a clock win, and the one that could be read that
way is the noisiest.**
That is exactly what should happen and it is a caution about reading Ir on
memset-shaped work. Callgrind charges `__memset_avx2_unaligned_erms`'s
`rep stosb` **one Ir per BYTE**; the hardware retires it at tens of bytes per
cycle. So 33.5 MB of dead zero-fill is ~33 M Ir (2.2 % of the frame's
instructions) but only ~1 M cycles (~0.2 % of the frame's 447.7 M cycles at
p6, using the stall record's cycle count). **The Ir ranking overstates a
zero-fill by roughly an order of magnitude relative to a change that removes
real instructions.** The resize is kept anyway — it is a 33.5 MB reduction in
per-frame memory traffic and a strictly-dead 2.10 % of the Ir ranking that
would otherwise keep drawing future chunks to it — but **no wall-clock gain is
claimed for it, and the honest reading is that the 1.018x-1.029x comes from the
three stack/scratch changes.** The other direction is worth stating too: those
three are worth ~1.07 % of Ir at p6 and read 1.019x on the clock, i.e. the
allocator's poor IPC (2.40 against the port's 3.44 frame average, per
`stall_attrib_2026-09-05`) makes them worth MORE in cycles than in
instructions.

