TWO EXTRA aarch64 CHECKS this change specifically needed:

  * **`SVTAV1_SC_TOOLS` still works.** The question-A sweep touched none of its
    code, and the tool is exercised rather than assumed: on
    `crop:gb82-sc/windows95.png 512x512 q48 p4`, unset = 8,521 B,
    `nopalette` = 12,465 B, `noibc` = 8,938 B, `none` = 14,000 B — four
    distinct sizes in the expected ordering (turning a screen tool off costs
    bytes), exactly as before.
  * **`arbitrary_size_robustness`: 128 / 128 panic-free + aomdec-decodable, 0
    refused.** This is the gate that matters for the two new `assert!`s
    (`MAX_EDGE_PX` in `extract_neighbors_tiled`, `MAX_TX_EDGE_PX` in
    `predict_unit_overlay`) — neither fires on any of its 128 odd geometries,
    and `video_key_matrix`'s SB128 cells exercise the 128-px edge itself.

A SECOND TRAP, worth recording: **`identity_full_8bit`'s verdict line was LOST
on the first aarch64 pass because the driver script piped it through
`| tail -3`, and a shell job-control line ("Done ...") landed in the last three
lines instead of the gate's own `1100 / 1100`.** The suite exited 0 and every
other step printed its verdict, so the run LOOKED complete. It was re-run with
the gate's full output captured to its own file. **Never `| tail -N` a gate
whose verdict you intend to quote** — capture the whole thing and grep for the
verdict line by name.

A NOTE ON A TRAP THIS CHUNK PAID FOR, for §5 of `docs/WORKING-ON-THIS.md`:
**a gate suite started before the last edit is a MIXED-BUILD run and its verdict
is worthless.** The first aarch64 pass was launched, then `pipeline.rs` was
edited and rebuilt while `screen_ibc_byte_gate` was still calling
`identity_run` (which runs `cargo build` per call). It was killed and re-run
from scratch against the final tree. The existing rule ("never rebuild Rust
while a sweep is using the binary") covers it; what is new is that the rebuild
does not have to be deliberate — an ordinary "fix one more thing" edit is
enough.

A CAVEAT ON THE PER-SITE BLOCK COUNTS, stated rather than hidden: **inlining
blurs DHAT's per-site attribution in a release build with no line tables.**
`hadamard_satd`'s two allocations appear under `inject_candidates` at one
program point and under `hadamard_satd` at another, so a SITES row can be off by
however much its callee was inlined into it. What is exact is the TOTALS
(244,967 blocks / 106,170,317 bytes before, 158,413 / 63,966,573 after), the
B-rows (taken from a `CARGO_PROFILE_RELEASE_DEBUG=1` build that resolves
file:line), and the measured Ir delta per change. Rank by the B-rows and by Ir,
not by the SITES block counts.

=================== 5b. THE Ir NUMBERS ARE THE COMMITTED BINARY'S =============

The Ir table in §4 was taken on a build that predates two late edits (moving a
`const` above a doc comment in `partition.rs`, and moving a misplaced
`#[allow(clippy::too_many_arguments)]` off a `const` and back onto its function
in `overlay.rs`). Both were argued to be codegen-neutral. **They were not, and
the argument was replaced by a measurement.**

First the floor: the SAME binary run twice on photo_cid p6 reads 1,463,905,845
and 1,463,903,039 Ir — a **~2,800-Ir (0.0002 %) run-to-run noise floor** from
process startup, not from the encode. Against that floor the committed binary
differs from the measured candidate by **44,556 Ir on photo_cid p6** (15x the
floor, in the FAVOURABLE direction) and by **137 Ir on screen_terminal p6**
(inside it). Item order feeds LLVM's inliner; a lint attribute does not, and
with two simultaneous edits there is no way to say which without another
bisect, which was not run.

The FIN rows in the TSV are the committed binary's, and the headline is quoted
from them. Every `.obu` from those runs was `cmp`'d against the candidate's and
is identical.

=================== 6. WHAT IS AND IS NOT ESTABLISHED =========================

ESTABLISHED, at commit 7ec5b5572 + this change, on the cells named above:
  * the per-call call counts (`getenv` 29/30/32, malloc/calloc/free/realloc) and
    the static census behind them — every `env::var` site in `crates/*/src` with
    its cache state and the rate of its enclosing function;
  * the ABSENCE of `Mutex`, `RwLock`, `HashMap`, `BTreeMap` and `Instant::now`
    from `crates/{svtav1-encoder,svtav1-dsp}/src` (a static fact from the sweep);
  * `sizeof` for eight C structures (identical on x86_64-linux and
    aarch64-macOS) and sixteen port structures;
  * DHAT's blocks / bytes / bytes-read / bytes-written per allocation site, for
    the port before and after and for C;
  * the callgrind Ir totals and the inclusive Ir of the allocator family;
  * the wall-clock ratios and their per-pair same-binary controls;
  * byte-identity on every cell measured and on the gate suites listed above.

NOT MEASURED, and not to be inferred:
  * any cycle counter for this change — the wall clock is the cycle evidence
    here, and no `perf stat` run was taken. The "33.5 MB is ~0.2 % of cycles"
    arithmetic in §4 uses `stall_attrib_2026-09-05`'s 447.7 M cycles/encode and
    a nominal ERMS store rate; it is an ORDER-OF-MAGNITUDE argument for why the
    Ir and the clock disagree, NOT a measurement of the resize's cycle cost;
  * aarch64 wall clock (a sibling lane, `zenav1-svt--mt1`, was live on that box
    the whole session). aarch64 peak RSS IS measured — see the MEM section;
  * 10-bit, inter frames, presets other than 2 and 6, qp other than 40, and the
    CLIC / line-art / 500x332 cells;
  * whether the remaining owned-`Vec`-return sites (§3's "not done" list) are
    worth their surgery — they are SIZED here, not priced against a fix;
  * the split of the port's excess branch instructions between bounds checks and
    loop control (unchanged from `stall_attrib_2026-09-05`: they cannot be
    separated by source line);
  * the `#[cfg(not(feature = "std"))]` arm of the new `with_hadamard_scratch`.
    It follows `tx_pipeline::with_txt_out`'s existing shape exactly, but it
    CANNOT be compiled today: `cargo check --no-default-features -p
    zenav1-svt-encoder` fails with **60 pre-existing errors** at this commit
    (`vec!`/`eprintln!` not in scope in `pipeline.rs:2691` and throughout
    `port_md/*` — none of them in a file this change touches), and
    `.github/workflows/rust-gates.yml` has no `--no-default-features` job. So
    the no-std build was already broken before this change and is not made worse
    by it, but the new arm is UNVERIFIED and is stated as such rather than
    claimed;
  * any `Option`-to-sentinel change — the 69 `saturating_sub` + 9 `checked_sub`
    sites in the encoder all compile through one `core` line and could not be
    priced individually, so none was attempted.

A CORRECTION TO THE BRIEF, stated because two of its premises were wrong:
  * "two `std::env::var("SVTAV1_SC_TOOLS")` calls at `pipeline.rs:11213` and
    `:11224` ... sit in the per-superblock funnel-config path" — the line
    numbers are stale (11599 / 11610 at this commit) and the path is per-TILE,
    not per-superblock. Measured: 29 `getenv` calls in the whole encode.
  * "`leaf_funnel/predict.rs:251`" is listed as an uncached candidate; it is the
    body of a `OnceLock::get_or_init` closure and was already cached, as is
    `depth_refine.rs:1357`.
  * The brief also asks for `benchmarks/stall_attrib_2026-09-05.meta` as
    required reading. **That file is not on `main@origin` and not in the primary
    checkout** — it exists only in the live `zenav1-svt--v4x1` workspace
    (`~/work/zen/zenav1-svt--v4x1/rust/benchmarks/`). It was read from there,
    read-only.
